<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tokugawa Mitsukuni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1628">1628</a>–<a href="/years/1701">1701</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Mito Komon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tokugawa Mitsukuni (1628–1701) was the second lord of the Mito domain and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. He began the compilation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-nihonshi">Dai Nihonshi</a>, a vast history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, which encouraged the Mito school of learning with its emphasis on loyalty to the emperor. He is popularly known as Mito Kōmon, hero of a long-running television drama.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-mitsukuni.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Mitsukuni" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokugawa_Mitsukuni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mitsukuni was born on 11 July 1628 in the Mito Domain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hitachi-province">Hitachi Province</a>, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yorifusa">Tokugawa Yorifusa</a>, the domain's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and the eleventh son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. His childhood name Chomaru was later changed to Chiyomatsu, a name granted by his cousin, the shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemitsu">Tokugawa Iemitsu</a>. When his elder brother Yorishige fell into poor health, Mitsukuni was chosen as heir, and he underwent the genpuku coming-of-age ceremony at nine. In 1657 he married a daughter of the kampaku Konoe Nobuhiro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of Mito in 1661. Anticipating the separation of kami and Buddhas ordered in 1868, he had a thousand Buddhist temples in the domain destroyed and ordered at least one shrine for every village. He gathered the Mitogaku scholars to compile the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-nihonshi">Dai Nihonshi</a>, which portrayed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as a nation under the emperor on the model of the Chinese dynasties, and which helped the growth of nationalism in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>. He also directed at Zuisen-ji the first guide to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>, the Shinpen Kamakurashi, whose names such as Kamakura's Seven Mouths and Ten Bridges are still used.</p><p>He received the court office of gon-chūnagon, provisional middle counsellor, and retired in 1691 to his villa Seizan-sō. Known as a gourmet, he is said to have been among the first Japanese to eat ramen and to have enjoyed wine and yogurt. His heir was Tokugawa Tsunaeda, the son of an elder brother, whom he adopted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mitsukuni died at Seizan-sō on 14 January 1701. He was given posthumous court ranks in 1869 and 1900 and is regarded as a kami. In the later Edo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji periods</a> a narrative tale, Mito Mitsukuni Man'yūki, invented journeys for him, and in 1951 television first showed him as a wanderer disguised as a commoner; the TBS series Mito Kōmon ran from 1969 to 2011. The city of Mito holds a Mito Komon festival each summer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tokugawa Mitsukuni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Tokugawa Mitsukuni</b> began the Dai Nihonshi, a history of Japan, in 1657; work on it continued until 1906 and gave rise to the Mito school, stressing loyalty to the emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/mito-domain">Mito Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mito-ibaraki">Mito</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.37,140.47&amp;year=1675" aria-label="Mito on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tokugawa Mitsukuni"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4347 4429h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4347 4429h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.37,140.47&amp;year=1675">What was here in 1675? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
